# [element].map(same).filter(keep);  changed = [other].map(alike); Object.setPrototypeOf(changed, Object.prototype); Array.prototype.filter.call(changed, take)
# What `map` answers is an array that a built-in has allocated, with the prototype object of arrays above it. It passes the test for a pristine array, so `filter` takes the overload for arrays alone:
# the elements go to `keep` and into the new array, nothing of them escapes, and nobody calls `element`.
# Given another prototype such an array may fail the test. The overload for any object is then applied too: the elements go to `take` and into the new array, and nobody calls `other`.
